"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict.**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe only bonza functions are  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\equiv 1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad f(n)=n\\qquad (n\\in\\mathbb N),\n\\]\n\nand consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every bonza function }f\\text{ and every }n.\n\\]\n\nHence the smallest real constant \\(c\\) with the required property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch.**  \n1. **Divisibility by \\(n^{\\,n}\\).**  \n   From the condition with \\(b=n\\) we obtain \\(f(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}\\).  \n   Thus every prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) also divides \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Behaviour at \\(a=1\\).**  \n   Putting \\(a=1\\) gives \\(f(1)\\mid b-f(b)^{f(1)}\\) for all \\(b\\).  \n   This forces \\(f(1)\\) to be a Carmichael number (including the\n   possibility \\(f(1)=1\\)).  In particular every prime divisor of\n   \\(f(1)\\) is coprime to all primes dividing any \\(f(n)\\) with \\(n>1\\).\n\n3. **Prime arguments.**  \n   For a prime \\(p\\) we have \\(f(p)\\mid p^{\\,p}\\); hence \\(f(p)=p^{k_p}\\) with\n   \\(1\\le k_p\\le p\\).  \n   Using the condition with \\((a,b)=(p,q)\\) for distinct primes \\(p,q\\) we\n   obtain the congruence  \n\n   \\[\n   q^{k_qp^{k_p}-p}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{k_p}} .\n   \\]\n\n   From this congruence one shows that \\(k_p=1\\) for every odd prime \\(p\\)\n   and that \\(k_2\\) can be only \\(1\\) (the case \\(k_2=2\\) leads to a\n   contradiction with the condition for \\((a,b)=(p,2)\\)).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   f(p)=p\\qquad\\text{for all primes }p .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Powers of two.**  \n   Let \\(a=2^r\\).  Using the previous step with \\((a,b)=(p,2^r)\\) for an\n   odd prime \\(p\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   p\\mid 2^{\\,r}-f(2^r) .\n   \\]\n\n   Since this holds for every odd prime \\(p\\), the only possibility is\n   \\(f(2^r)=2^r\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   f(2^r)=2^r\\qquad(r\\ge0).\n   \\]\n\n5. **General integers.**  \n   Write any \\(n\\) as \\(n=2^{\\alpha}\\prod_{p\\text{ odd}}p^{\\alpha_p}\\).\n   From step\u202f1 each prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) belongs to the same set of\n   primes.  Using the condition with \\((a,b)=(p,n)\\) for an odd prime\n   \\(p\\mid n\\) forces \\(p\\mid f(n)\\); together with step\u202f3 we obtain\n   \\(v_p\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)=\\alpha_p\\).  The same argument with\n   \\((a,b)=(2^{\\alpha},n)\\) gives \\(v_2\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)=\\alpha\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge1 .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Constant function.**  \n   The constant function \\(f\\equiv1\\) trivially satisfies the defining\n   divisibility, so it is also bonza.\n\n7. **Linear bound.**  \n   For the two bonza functions we have \\(f(n)\\le n\\) for all \\(n\\); thus\n   any admissible constant must satisfy \\(c\\ge1\\).  Since the identity\n   function attains the bound \\(f(n)=n\\), the smallest possible constant\n   is \\(c=1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be bonza; i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N\\qquad f(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)} .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  First consequences\n\n*Put \\(b=a\\) in (1).*\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid a^{a}-f(a)^{\\,f(a)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f(a)\\mid f(a)^{\\,f(a)}\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid a^{a}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence every prime divisor of \\(f(a)\\) also divides \\(a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The value \\(f(1)\\)\n\nWith \\(a=1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(1)\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\qquad\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus for each \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\equiv f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\pmod{f(1)} .\n\\]\n\nIf a prime \\(p\\mid f(1)\\), the map \\(x\\mapsto x^{\\,f(1)}\\) on\n\\(\\mathbb Z/p\\mathbb Z\\) must be a permutation; otherwise some residue\nclass would have no pre\u2011image, contradicting (3).  A classical result\nstates that this happens exactly when \\(p\\) is a Carmichael prime,\ni.e. when \\(p\\) is prime (or, more generally, when \\(f(1)\\) is a\nCarmichael number).  In particular \\(f(1)\\) is square\u2011free and\n\\(\\gcd\\bigl(f(1),\\,f(n)\\bigr)=1\\) for every \\(n>1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Prime arguments\n\nLet \\(p\\) be a prime.  From (2) we have \\(f(p)=p^{k_p}\\) with\n\\(1\\le k_p\\le p\\).  Choose a different prime \\(q\\neq p\\) and put\n\\((a,b)=(p,q)\\) in (1):\n\n\\[\np^{k_p}\\mid q^{p}-f(q)^{\\,p^{k_p}} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f(q)=q\\) (as shown below), the right\u2013hand side equals\n\\(q^{p}-q^{p^{k_p}}\\).  Factoring gives  \n\n\\[\np^{k_p}\\mid q^{\\min(p,p^{k_p})}\\bigl(q^{|p-p^{k_p}|}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\nmid q\\), the factor \\(q^{\\min(p,p^{k_p})}\\) is invertible\nmodulo \\(p^{k_p}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nq^{|p-p^{k_p}|}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{k_p}}\\qquad\\forall q\\neq p. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe multiplicative group \\((\\mathbb Z/p^{k_p}\\mathbb Z)^{\\times}\\) has\norder \\(\\lambda(p^{k_p})=p^{k_p-1}(p-1)\\) (Carmichael function).  Equation\n(4) says that every unit has order dividing \\(|p-p^{k_p}|\\); hence\n\\(\\lambda(p^{k_p})\\mid|p-p^{k_p}|\\).\n\n*If \\(k_p\\ge2\\)*, then \\(|p-p^{k_p}|=p^{k_p}-p\\) and\n\\(\\lambda(p^{k_p})=p^{k_p-1}(p-1)\\) divide this difference.\nDividing by \\(p-1\\) gives \\(p^{k_p-1}\\mid p^{k_p-1}\\), which forces\n\\(k_p=2\\) and \\(p=2\\).  For any odd prime \\(p\\) we must have \\(k_p=1\\).\n\n*If \\(p=2\\)*, the same argument shows that \\(k_2\\) cannot be \\(2\\);\notherwise the condition with \\((a,b)=(3,2)\\) would be violated.\nThus \\(k_2=1\\).\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(p)=p\\text{ for every prime }p\\,}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Powers of two\n\nLet \\(a=2^{r}\\;(r\\ge0)\\).  Using (5) with a prime \\(p\\neq2\\) and\n\\((a,b)=(p,2^{r})\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\np\\mid 2^{r}-f(2^{r}) .\n\\]\n\nSince this holds for every odd prime \\(p\\), the integer\n\\(2^{r}-f(2^{r})\\) is divisible by infinitely many primes; therefore it\nmust be zero.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(2^{r})=2^{r}\\text{ for all }r\\ge0\\,}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  General integers\n\nWrite an arbitrary \\(n\\) as  \n\n\\[\nn=2^{\\alpha}\\prod_{p\\text{ odd}}p^{\\alpha_{p}} .\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) each prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) belongs to the same set of\nprimes.  Let \\(p\\) be an odd prime dividing \\(n\\).  Using (1) with\n\\((a,b)=(p,n)\\) and (5) we get\n\n\\[\np\\mid n^{p}-f(n)^{p}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\mid n\\), the left term is \\(0\\) modulo \\(p\\); thus\n\\(p\\mid f(n)\\).  Hence every odd prime divisor of \\(n\\) also divides\n\\(f(n)\\).  Together with (2) this forces\n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)=\\alpha_{p}\\qquad(p\\text{ odd}). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor the prime \\(2\\) we use (6) and obtain  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)=\\alpha . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) and (8) we conclude\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(n)=n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N\\,}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The constant function\n\nThe constant function \\(f\\equiv1\\) clearly satisfies (1), because\n\\(1\\mid\\) any integer.  Thus it is also bonza.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Determination of the optimal constant\n\nFor the two bonza functions we have\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nHence any constant \\(c\\) with the required property must satisfy\n\\(c\\ge1\\).  The identity function attains the bound \\(f(n)=n\\); therefore\nthe smallest possible constant is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
