"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict.**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe smallest real constant satisfying the required inequality for all bonza functions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch.**  \n1. **\\(f(1)=1\\).**  \n   Assume \\(f(1)=m>1\\) and let \\(p\\mid m\\).  \n   From the defining condition with \\((a,b)=(1,p)\\) we obtain \\(p\\mid f(p)\\);  \n   from \\((a,b)=(p,1)\\) we obtain \\(p\\mid m^{p^{k}}-1\\) where \\(f(p)=p^{k}\\).  \n   This is impossible because \\(p\\mid m\\) forces \\(m^{p^{k}}\\equiv0\\pmod p\\).  \n   Hence \\(m=1\\).\n\n2. **Odd arguments.**  \n   For any odd prime \\(p\\) we have \\(f(p)=p^{k}\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le p\\).  \n   Using \\((a,b)=(p,2)\\) and the fact that \\(f(2)\\mid4\\) (from \\(f(2)\\mid2^{2}\\)) we show that\n   \\(k=0\\); thus \\(f(p)=1\\).  \n   For an odd composite \\(n\\) pick an odd prime divisor \\(p\\mid n\\); then\n   \\(f(n)\\mid p^{\\,n}-1\\) (by \\((a,b)=(n,p)\\)) and also \\(f(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}\\) (by the definition).  \n   Since \\(p^{\\,n}-1\\) is even but not divisible by any odd prime dividing \\(n\\),\n   the only possible odd divisor of \\(f(n)\\) is \\(1\\); the even part must be a power of \\(2\\),\n   but \\(n^{\\,n}\\) is odd, so the even part is forced to be \\(1\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=1\\qquad\\text{for every odd }n.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Even arguments.**  \n   Write an even integer as \\(n=2^{\\alpha}m\\) with \\(\\alpha\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\) odd.  \n   From step\u202f2, \\(f(m)=1\\).  Using \\((a,b)=(n,m)\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid m^{\\,n}-1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For any odd \\(t\\) we also have \\(f(t)=1\\), so \\((a,b)=(n,t)\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid t^{\\,n}-1\\qquad\\forall\\;t\\text{ odd}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   By the Lifting\u2011the\u2011Exponent Lemma  \n\n   \\[\n   v_{2}\\!\\bigl(t^{\\,n}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(t-1)+v_{2}(t+1)+\\alpha-1\\ge\\alpha+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every odd \\(t\\) satisfies \\(2^{\\alpha+2}\\mid t^{\\,n}-1\\), and the\n   greatest common divisor of the numbers in (2) equals \\(2^{\\alpha+2}\\).  \n   From (2) we conclude  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid2^{\\alpha+2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(f(n)\\) is a power of \\(2\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\le2^{\\alpha+2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(f(2)\\mid2^{2}=4\\), the bound (4) is sharp for \\(\\alpha=1\\) (we have\n   \\(f(2)\\le4\\)).\n\n4. **Upper bound for the ratio.**  \n   For any \\(n=2^{\\alpha}m\\) (\\(m\\) odd) we have from (4)\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{f(n)}{n}\\le\\frac{2^{\\alpha+2}}{2^{\\alpha}m}\n   =\\frac{4}{m}\\le4 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le4n\\) for all \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   f(1)=1,\\qquad\n   f(n)=1\\;(n\\text{ odd}),\\qquad\n   f(2)=4,\\qquad\n   f\\!\\bigl(2^{\\alpha}m\\bigr)=2^{\\alpha+2}\\;( \\alpha\\ge2,\\;m\\text{ odd}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   - If both arguments are odd, the condition is trivial because \\(f\\equiv1\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\) is odd and \\(b\\) arbitrary, \\(f(a)=1\\) divides everything.  \n   - Let \\(a=2^{\\alpha}m\\) (\\(\\alpha\\ge1\\)).  \n     \u2022 For odd \\(b\\) we have \\(f(b)=1\\) and, by the LTE computation above,\n       \\(2^{\\alpha+2}\\mid b^{\\,a}-1\\); hence \\(f(a)\\mid b^{\\,a}-1\\).  \n     \u2022 For even \\(b=2^{\\beta}k\\) (\\(\\beta\\ge1\\), \\(k\\) odd) we have\n       \\(f(b)=2^{\\beta+2}\\) (or \\(4\\) when \\(\\beta=1\\)).  \n       Both terms \\(b^{\\,a}\\) and \\(f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\) are powers of \\(2\\) multiplied\n       by odd factors; the exponent of \\(2\\) in each term is at least\n       \\(\\min(\\beta a,\\;(\\beta+2)f(a))\\ge\\alpha+2\\).  Hence their difference is\n       divisible by \\(2^{\\alpha+2}=f(a)\\).  \n   Thus the function satisfies the defining divisibility for all \\(a,b\\).\n\n   For \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\) we have \\(f(2^{\\alpha})=2^{\\alpha+2}\\), so\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{f(2^{\\alpha})}{2^{\\alpha}}=4,\n   \\]\n\n   showing that the constant \\(4\\) cannot be improved.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le4n\\), and the constructed example\n   attains the ratio \\(4\\).  Hence the smallest admissible constant is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4}.\n   \\]"
