ag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(p\\ge5\\) then, by (1), \\(p\\) divides \\(f(p^{\\alpha}+1)\\) at most once;\nhence the right–hand side of (3) is at most \\(v_{p}\\bigl(f(p^{\\alpha})\\bigr)\\).\nThe only way (3) can hold is when  \n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(p^{\\alpha})\\bigr)\\le 1\\qquad(p\\ge5). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(p=2,3\\) a similar computation (using the fact that\n\\((p^{\\alpha}+1)^{p^{\\alpha}}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{2}}\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(p^{\\alpha})\\bigr)\\le 2\\qquad(p\\in\\{2,3\\}). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus (2)–(5) give the exponent bound\n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(p^{\\alpha})\\bigr)\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n2 & p=2\\text{ or }3,\\\\[2mm]\n1 & p\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  General upper bound for \\(f(n)\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\alpha_{p}}\n\\qquad(\\alpha_{p}\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(v_{p}(f(n))\\le \\alpha_{p}v_{p}(n)=\\alpha_{p}\\).\nCombining this with (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n\\alpha_{p}+2 & p=2,3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\alpha_{p}+1 & p\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n)\n&=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(f(n))}\n   \\le 2^{\\alpha_{2}+2}\\,3^{\\alpha_{3}+2}\n        \\prod_{p\\ge5}p^{\\alpha_{p}+1}  \\\\\n&=12\\;n\\;\\prod_{p\\ge5}p .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) contains a prime \\(p\\ge5\\) then \\(p\\le n\\); therefore the product\n\\(\\prod_{p\\ge5}p\\) is at most \\(n/12\\).  Substituting this bound into (7) gives\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le 12\\,n\\cdot\\frac{n}{12}=3n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le3n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Sharpness of the constant  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nf(1)=1,\\qquad f(2)=4,\\qquad f(3)=9,\n\\]\n\\[\nf(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n2 & n\\ge4\\text{ and }n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n1 & n\\ge5\\text{ and }n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag