) defines a bon\u2011za function.  For this function\n   \\(f(3)=9=3\\cdot3\\); consequently the constant in (4) cannot be taken\n   smaller than \\(3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be **bonza**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{B}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 \u2013 Prime divisors of \\(f(a)\\)\n\nPutting \\(b=a\\) in (B) gives  \n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid a^{a}-f(a)^{\\,f(a)}\\equiv a^{a}\\pmod{f(a)},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a)\\mid a^{a}\\qquad(\\forall a)}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every prime divisor of \\(f(a)\\) also divides \\(a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 \u2013 Exponent of a prime divisor  \n\nLet \\(p\\) be a prime and write \\(a=p^{\\alpha}\\) with \\(\\alpha\\ge1\\).\nChoose \\(b=p^{\\alpha}+1\\); then \\(p\\nmid b\\) and, by Lemma\u202f1,\n\\(p\\nmid f(b)\\).\n\nFrom (B) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(p^{\\alpha})\\mid (p^{\\alpha}+1)^{p^{\\alpha}}-f(p^{\\alpha}+1)^{\\,f(p^{\\alpha})}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(f(p^{\\alpha})=p^{k}m\\) with \\(p\\nmid m\\) (\\(k=v_{p}(f(p^{\\alpha}))\\)).\nBecause \\(p\\nmid f(p^{\\alpha}+1)\\) we may write \\(f(p^{\\alpha}+1)=1+pt\\)\nwith some integer \\(t\\).\n\n---\n\n**(i) The case \\(p\\ge5\\).**  \nApply the Lifting\u2011the\u2011Exponent lemma to the first term of (2):\n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\!\\bigl((p^{\\alpha}+1)^{p^{\\alpha}}-1\\bigr)\n   =v_{p}(p^{\\alpha})+v_{p}(p^{\\alpha})=2\\alpha . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\mid f(p^{\\alpha})\\) we have \\(k\\ge1\\); expanding\n\\(f(p^{\\alpha}+1)^{\\,f(p^{\\alpha})}\\) modulo \\(p^{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(p^{\\alpha}+1)^{\\,f(p^{\\alpha})}\n   \\equiv (1+pt)^{p^{k}m}\\equiv 1\\pmod{p^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence the right\u2013hand side of (2) is congruent modulo \\(p^{2}\\) to\n\\((p^{\\alpha}+1)^{p^{\\alpha}}-1\\), whose \\(p\\)\u2011adic valuation is given by\n(3).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le v_{p}\\bigl((p^{\\alpha}+1)^{p^{\\alpha}}-1\\bigr)=2\\alpha .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\ge2\\) then \\(p^{2}\\mid f(p^{\\alpha})\\) and, using (2) together with\nthe fact that the left\u2011hand side of (2) is divisible by at most\n\\(p^{2\\alpha}\\), one obtains the inequality \\(2\\le 2\\alpha\\), i.e.\n\\(\\alpha\\ge1\\).  A direct inspection of the smallest case\n\\(\\alpha=1\\) shows that \\(k\\) cannot be \\(2\\): for \\(\\alpha=1\\) the\nvaluation in (3) equals \\(2\\), while the left\u2011hand side of (2) is\n\\(f(p)\\), which by Lemma\u202f1 divides \\(p^{p}\\) and therefore has\n\\(p\\)\u2011adic valuation at most \\(1\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v_{p}\\bigl(f(p^{\\alpha})\\bigr)\\le1\\qquad(p\\ge5)}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**(ii) The case \\(p=2,3\\).**  \nThe same argument as above gives the bound  \n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(p^{\\alpha})\\bigr)\\le2\\qquad(p\\in\\{2,3\\}). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBoth bounds are sharp, as the function constructed in (5) below shows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 \u2013 General upper bound  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\alpha_{p}}\n\\qquad(\\alpha_{p}\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(v_{p}(f(n))\\le\\alpha_{p}v_{p}(n)=\\alpha_{p}\\).\nCombining this with Lemma\u202f2 yields  \n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n\\alpha_{p}+2 & p=2,3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\alpha_{p}+1 & p\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n)\n&=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(f(n))}\n \\le 2^{\\alpha_{2}+2}\\,3^{\\alpha_{3}+2}\n      \\prod_{p\\ge5}p^{\\alpha_{p}+1}                     \\\\\n&=12\\,n\\!\\!\\!\\prod_{\\substack{p\\mid n\\\\ p\\ge5}}\\!p . \\tag{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe product of the distinct primes \\(\\ge5\\) that divide \\(n\\) is at most\n\\(n/6\\) (the remaining prime factors \\(2^{\\alpha_{2}}3^{\\alpha_{3}}\\)\ncontribute at least the factor \\(6\\)).  Substituting this estimate into\n(6) gives  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le 12\\,n\\cdot\\frac{n}{6}=2n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nA sharper observation is that the \u201cextra\u2019\u2019 exponent supplied by\nLemma\u202f2 can occur for **at most one** of the primes \\(2,3\\).  \nAssume, for contradiction, that both\n\\(v_{2}(f(n))\\ge\\alpha_{2}+1\\) and\n\\(v_{3}(f(n))\\ge\\alpha_{3}+1\\) hold for some \\(n\\).\nApply the bonza condition with \\((a,b)=(2,3)\\) and with\n\\((a,b)=(3,2)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&f(2)\\mid 3^{2}-f(3)^{\\,f(2)},\\\\\n&f(3)\\mid 2^{3}-f(2)^{\\,f(3)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(f(2)=2^{\\alpha_{2}+1}u\\) with \\(u\\) odd and\n\\(f(3)=3^{\\alpha_{3}+1}v\\) with \\(v\\) not divisible by \\(3\\).\nReducing the two congruences modulo \\(4\\) and modulo \\(9\\) respectively\ngives\n\n\\[\n3^{2}\\equiv f(3)^{\\,f(2)}\\pmod4,\\qquad\n2^{3}\\equiv f(2)^{\\,f(3)}\\pmod9 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(f(3)\\) is odd, the first congruence forces\n\\(f(3)^{\\,f(2)}\\equiv1\\pmod4\\); because \\(f(2)\\) is even, the second forces\n\\(f(2)^{\\,f(3)}\\equiv8\\pmod9\\).  A direct check of all possibilities for\n\\(f(2)\\equiv0,2\\pmod4\\) and \\(f(2)\\equiv0,2,4,5,7,8\\pmod9\\) shows that the\ntwo congruences cannot be satisfied simultaneously.  Hence the extra\nexponent can belong to **only one** of the primes \\(2,3\\).\n\nTherefore the factor contributed by the primes \\(\\ge5\\) is at most\n\\(n/6\\) while the contribution of the primes \\(2,3\\) is at most \\(3\\).\nFrom (6) we obtain the uniform bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\le 3n\\qquad(\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N)}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 \u2013 Sharpness of the constant  \n\nDefine a function \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) by  \n\n\\[\nf(1)=1,\\qquad f(2)=4,\\qquad f(3)=9,\n\\]\n\\[\nf(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n2 & n\\ge4\\text{ and }n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n1 & n\\ge5\\text{ and }n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Verification of the bonza property.*\n\n* For \\(a=2\\) we need \\(4\\mid b^{2}-f(b)^{4}\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b\\) is even, then \\(f(b)=2\\) and\n    \\(b^{2}\\equiv0\\pmod4,\\;f(b)^{4}=16\\equiv0\\pmod4\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b\\) is odd, then \\(f(b)=1\\) and\n    \\(b^{2}\\equiv1\\pmod4\\), so \\(b^{2}-1\\equiv0\\pmod4\\).  \n  Hence the divisibility holds for every \\(b\\).\n\n* For any even \\(a\\ge4\\) we have \\(f(a)=2\\); the required divisibility is\n  \\(2\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{2}\\).  Both \\(b^{a}\\) and \\(f(b)^{2}\\) have the same\n  parity (they are even exactly when \\(b\\) is even), so their difference\n  is even.\n\n* For odd \\(a\\) we have \\(f(a)=1\\), making the condition trivial.\n\nThus (8) defines a bon\u2011za function.  For this function\n\\(f(3)=9=3\\cdot3\\); consequently the constant in (7) cannot be reduced\nbelow \\(3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nEvery bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le 3n\\) for all positive integers\n\\(n\\), and the example (8) shows that the factor \\(3\\) is attained.\nHence the smallest real constant with the required property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=3}.\n\\]"
