b‑case).  From (B) with \\((a,b)=(p,n_{0})\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np\\mid n_{0}^{p}-f(n_{0})^{p}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\mid n_{0}\\), the congruence reduces to  \n\n\\[\np\\mid f(n_{0}) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus we may write  \n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})=p\\,t\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb N). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Using \\((a,b)=(n_{0},p)\\)\n\nNow apply (B) with \\((a,b)=(n_{0},p)\\).  Using (5) and the fact that\n\\(f(p)=p\\) (by (3) because \\(p<n_{0}\\)), we get  \n\n\\[\np\\,t\\mid p^{\\,n_{0}}-p^{\\,p t}\n      =p^{\\,n_{0}}\\bigl(1-p^{\\,p t-n_{0}}\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(t=1\\) then \\(f(n_{0})=p=n_{0}\\) (because \\(p\\) is the smallest\nprime divisor of \\(n_{0}\\)), contradicting the choice of \\(n_{0}\\).\nHence \\(t\\ge 2\\) and consequently \\(p t>n_{0}\\); the exponent\n\\(p t-n_{0}\\) in (6) is a positive integer.\n\nThe factor \\(1-p^{\\,p t-n_{0}}\\) is **not** divisible by \\(p\\); therefore\nthe exact power of \\(p\\) dividing the right–hand side of (6) is\n\\(p^{\\,n_{0}}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(n_{0})\\bigr)=v_{p}(p t)\\le n_{0}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Prime divisors different from \\(p\\)\n\nLet \\(q\\neq p\\) be a prime divisor of \\(f(n_{0})\\).  Reducing (6) modulo\n\\(q\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n0\\equiv p^{\\,n_{0}}\\bigl(1-p^{\\,p t-n_{0}}\\bigr)\\pmod q .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(q\\neq p\\), the factor \\(p^{\\,n_{0}}\\) is invertible modulo\n\\(q\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nq\\mid 1-p^{\\,p t-n_{0}} . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe integer on the right–hand side of (8) is strictly smaller than\n\\(p^{\\,p t-n_{0}}\\).  Hence every prime divisor \\(q\\neq p\\) of\n\\(f(n_{0})\\) is bounded by a constant that depends only on the positive\ninteger \\(p t-n_{0}\\) (and therefore on \\(f(n_{0})-n_{0}\\)), **not** on\n\\(n_{0}\\) itself.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final contradiction\n\nFrom (7) we have  \n\n\\[\np^{\\,v_{p}(f(n_{0}))}\\le p^{\\,n_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nTogether with the bound for the remaining prime factors obtained from\n(8) we deduce the existence of a constant \\(C\\) (independent of\n\\(n_{0}\\)) such that  \n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})\\le p^{\\,n_{0}}\\cdot C . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBut (6) tells us that \\(f(n_{0})\\) divides the number\n\\(p^{\\,n_{0}}(1-p^{\\,p t-n_{0}})\\).  The factor\n\\(1-p^{\\,p t-n_{0}}\\) is **coprime** to \\(p\\); therefore any divisor of\nthe right–hand side must be of the form \\(p^{\\,\\alpha}\\) with\n\\(\\alpha\\le n_{0}\\).  Consequently the only possible value for\n\\(f(n_{0})\\) compatible with (6) is \\(f(n_{0})=p^{\\,\\alpha}\\) for some\n\\(\\alpha\\le n_{0}\\).\n\nIf \\(\\alpha<n_{0}\\) then \\(f(n_{0})<n_{0}\\), contradicting the definition\nof \\(n_{0}\\).  Hence \\(\\alpha=n_{0}\\) and \\(f(n_{0})=p^{\\,n_{0}}\\).\nBut \\(p^{\\,n_{0}}\\) is a multiple of \\(p\\) strictly larger than \\(n_{0}\\)\nunless \\(n_{0}=p\\).  The case \\(n_{0}=p\\) would give \\(f(p)=p\\), which\nagain contradicts the assumption \\(f(p)\\neq p\\).\n\nThus no such minimal counter‑example \\(n_{0}\\) can exist.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=n\\quad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Determination of the constant \\(c\\)\n\nSince every bonza function is the identity, we have \\(f(n)=n\\) and\ntherefore \\(f(n)\\le n\\) for all \\(n\\).  The constant \\(c=1\\) works for\nall bonza functions.  The identity function itself shows that no smaller\nconstant can work.  Consequently the smallest real constant satisfying\nthe required property is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
