-----------------------\n#### 2.  An exponent bound for an odd prime\n\nFix an odd prime \\(p\\) and let  \n\n\\[\np^{k}\\parallel n,\\qquad p^{t}\\parallel f(n) .\n\\]\n\nAssume, towards a contradiction, that \\(t>k\\).\n\nChoose any integer \\(b\\) with \\(\\gcd(b,p)=1\\).  \nBecause of the remark after (1), also \\(\\gcd\\bigl(f(b),p\\bigr)=1\\).\nApplying (B) with the pair \\((a,b)=(n,b)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np^{t}\\mid b^{\\,n}-f(b)^{\\,p^{t}} . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(b\\) and \\(f(b)\\) are units modulo \\(p^{t}\\); dividing (2) by the unit\n\\(f(b)^{\\,p^{t}}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(b\\,f(b)^{-1}\\bigr)^{\\,n}\\equiv 1\\pmod{p^{t}} . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(b\\) runs through the integers coprime to \\(p\\), the residue\n\\(u:=b\\,f(b)^{-1}\\) runs through the whole multiplicative group\n\\((\\mathbb Z/p^{t}\\mathbb Z)^{\\times}\\).  Hence (3) forces  \n\n\\[\nu^{\\,n}\\equiv 1\\pmod{p^{t}}\\qquad\\forall u\\in(\\mathbb Z/p^{t}\\mathbb Z)^{\\times}.\n\\]\n\nThe exponent of this group is the Carmichael function  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda(p^{t})=p^{t-1}(p-1).\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\np^{t-1}(p-1)\\mid n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p^{k}\\parallel n\\) and \\(p\\nmid(p-1)\\), (4) implies \\(p^{t-1}\\mid p^{k}\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\nt-1\\le k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThus for every odd prime \\(p\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\nu _p\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\le \\nu _p(n)\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The prime \\(2\\)\n\nRepeating the previous argument with \\(p=2\\) we obtain the same conclusion,\nexcept that the factor \\(p-1\\) disappears from (4).  Consequently we get  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\nu _2\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\le \\nu _2(n)+1\\;}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Linear upper bound for every bonza function\n\nWrite the prime factorisation of \\(n\\) as  \n\n\\[\nn=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\nu _p(n)} .\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) and (6),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n)&=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\nu _p\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)}\n      \\le 2^{\\nu _2(n)+1}\\!\\!\\!\\prod_{\\substack{p\\mid n\\\\p\\text{ odd}}}\n          p^{\\nu _p(n)}  \\\\\n    &=2\\;n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence every bonza function satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\le 2n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Sharpness of the constant \\(2\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n2n, & n=2^{k}\\;(k\\ge 0),\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(f\\) is bonza.\n\n*If \\(a\\) is odd.*  Then \\(f(a)=a\\).  For any \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\nb^{a}-f(b)^{\\,a}=b^{a}-b^{a}=0,\n\\]\n\nso the divisibility holds.\n\n*If \\(a=2^{k}\\) ( \\(k\\ge 0\\) ).*  Then \\(f(a)=2^{k+1}\\).\nLet \\(b\\) be arbitrary.\n\n- If \\(b\\) is odd, Euler\u2019s theorem gives \\(b^{2^{k}}\\equiv1\\pmod{2^{k+1}}\\).\n  Moreover \\(f(b)=b\\) is odd, so\n  \\(f(b)^{\\,2^{k+1}}=b^{\\,2^{k+1}}\\equiv b^{\\,2^{k}}\\equiv1\\pmod{2^{k+1}}\\).\n  Hence the difference is a multiple of \\(2^{k+1}=f(a)\\).\n\n- If \\(b\\) is even, write \\(b=2^{r}c\\) with \\(c\\) odd.\n  Then both \\(b^{2^{k}}\\) and \\(f(b)^{\\,2^{k+1}}\\) contain the factor\n  \\(2^{r2^{k}}\\ge 2^{k+1}\\); consequently their difference is also divisible\n  by \\(2^{k+1}\\).\n\nThus (B) holds for all \\(a,b\\); the function (9) is bonza and attains\n\\(f(2^{k})=2\\cdot 2^{k}=2n\\).  Therefore the constant \\(2\\) cannot be reduced.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nEvery bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le 2n\\), and the bound is attained.\nHence the smallest real constant with the required property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]"
