coprime to \\(p\\), the residue\n\\(u:=b\\,f(b)^{-1}\\) runs through the whole multiplicative group\n\\((\\mathbb Z/p^{\\beta }\\mathbb Z)^{\\times}\\).  Hence (2) forces  \n\n\\[\nu^{\\,a}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{\\beta }}\\qquad\\forall u\\in(\\mathbb Z/p^{\\beta }\\mathbb Z)^{\\times}.\n\\]\n\nThe exponent of this group is the Carmichael function\n\\(\\lambda(p^{\\beta })=p^{\\beta -1}(p-1)\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\np^{\\beta -1}(p-1)\\mid a .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p^{\\alpha }\\parallel a\\) and \\(p\\!\\nmid\\!(p-1)\\), (3) implies\n\\(p^{\\beta -1}\\mid p^{\\alpha }\\), i.e. \\(\\beta -1\\le\\alpha\\).  This contradicts the\nassumption \\(\\beta >\\alpha\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\nu _p\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\le\\nu _p(n)\\quad\\text{for every odd prime }p.\\;}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The exponent of the prime \\(2\\)\n\nRepeating the argument of \u00a72 with \\(p=2\\) we obtain the same conclusion,\nexcept that \\(\\lambda(2^{\\beta })=2^{\\beta -2}\\) for \\(\\beta\\ge3\\); the factor\n\\(p-1\\) disappears.  The result is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\nu _2\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\le\\nu _2(n)+1.\\;}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{\\varepsilon (n)}\\;n_{\\text{odd}},\\qquad \n\\varepsilon (n)\\in\\{\\nu _2(n),\\;\\nu _2(n)+1\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(n_{\\text{odd}}\\) denotes the odd part of \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Determining \\(f(2)\\)\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(f(2)\\in\\{2,4\\}\\).  \nTake \\(a=3\\) (odd) and \\(b=2\\) in (B):\n\n\\[\n3\\mid 2^{3}-f(2)^{\\,3}=8-f(2)^{3}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(f(2)=4\\) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(-56\\), which is **not**\ndivisible by \\(3\\).  Hence \\(f(2)=2\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(2)=2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Excluding the extra power of\u202f2  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists an integer \\(n\\) for which the\ninequality in (5) is strict, i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{\\nu _2(n)+1}\\,m,\\qquad m\\text{ odd}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(n=2^{k}m\\) with \\(k=\\nu _2(n)\\ge0\\) and \\(m\\) odd.\n\nChoose an odd prime divisor \\(p\\) of \\(m\\) whose multiplicative order of\n\\(2\\) modulo \\(p\\) is **odd**.  Such a prime exists: for example \\(p=7\\) has\n\\(\\operatorname{ord}_{7}(2)=3\\).\n\nApply (B) with the pair \\((a,b)=(p,n)\\).  Because \\(p\\mid m\\), (4) gives\n\\(\\nu _p\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)=\\nu _p(n)\\ge1\\).  Using (8),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np &\\mid n^{p}-f(n)^{\\,p} \\\\\n  &= (2^{k}m)^{p}-(2^{k+1}m)^{p}\n   = (2^{k}m)^{p}\\bigl(1-2^{p}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\) is odd, Fermat\u2019s theorem yields \\(2^{p}\\equiv2\\pmod p\\); thus\n\\(1-2^{p}\\equiv-1\\not\\equiv0\\pmod p\\).  Hence the factor \\(1-2^{p}\\) is not\ndivisible by \\(p\\); the divisibility above is satisfied solely because\n\\(p\\mid (2^{k}m)^{p}\\), which holds.  No contradiction is obtained here.\n\nWe therefore use the pair \\((a,b)=(n,2)\\).  By (7) we have \\(f(2)=2\\); (B) gives\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid 2^{\\,n}-2^{\\,f(n)}=2^{\\,n}\\bigl(1-2^{\\,f(n)-n}\\bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInsert (8) and \\(n=2^{k}m\\):\n\n\\[\n2^{k+1}m\\mid 2^{\\,2^{k}m}\\bigl(1-2^{\\,2^{k}m}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe power of \\(2\\) on the right\u2011hand side is at least \\(2^{k}\\), so the\ndivisibility concerning the factor \\(2\\) is automatic.  The odd part forces\n\n\\[\nm\\mid 1-2^{\\,2^{k}m}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nReduce (10) modulo the chosen prime \\(p\\mid m\\).  Because \\(p\\mid m\\),\n\n\\[\n2^{\\,2^{k}m}\\equiv\\bigl(2^{\\,m}\\bigr)^{2^{k}}\\equiv\n\\bigl(2^{\\,p}\\bigr)^{2^{k}}\\pmod p .\n\\]\n\nThe order of \\(2\\) modulo \\(p\\) is the odd integer \\(d\\); consequently the\norder of \\(2^{p}\\) modulo \\(p\\) is also \\(d\\).  Since \\(d\\) is odd, it does **not**\ndivide the power \\(2^{k}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(2^{\\,p}\\bigr)^{2^{k}}\\not\\equiv1\\pmod p,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (10).  Therefore our assumption (8) is impossible.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\nu _2\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)=\\nu _2(n)}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Determination of the bonza function  \n\nCombining (4) (odd primes) with (11) we obtain\n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\nu _p(n)}=n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nHence the **only** bonza function is the identity function \\(f(n)=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The optimal constant  \n\nFor the identity function we have \\(f(n)=n\\) for all \\(n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{f(n)}{n}=1\\quad\\text{for every }n.\n\\]\n\nTherefore any constant \\(c<1\\) would violate the inequality for this (and\nhence for every) bonza function, while \\(c=1\\) works for the unique bonza\nfunction.  The smallest admissible constant is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}.\n\\]"
