f(n)\\).*  \n   Suppose a prime \\(r\\neq p\\) divides \\(f(n)\\).  \n   Take \\(b=r\\) (so \\(f(r)=r\\) by the prime case) and the pair \\((a,b)=(n,r)\\).  \n   The divisibility condition reads  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid r^{\\,n}-r^{\\,f(n)}=r^{\\,\\min (n,f(n))}\\bigl(r^{|n-f(n)|}-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(r\\nmid r^{|n-f(n)|}-1\\), the factor \\(r^{\\min (n,f(n))}\\) must contain the whole power of \\(r\\) occurring in \\(f(n)\\).  \n   Hence \\(r^{\\nu_{r}(f(n))}\\mid r^{\\min (n,f(n))}\\), which forces \\(\\nu_{r}(f(n))\\le \\min (n,f(n))\\).  \n   Because \\(r\\neq p\\) and \\(r\\mid n\\) would contradict the induction hypothesis (all proper divisors of \\(n\\) already satisfy \\(f(\\cdot)=\\cdot\\)), we must have \\(r\\nmid n\\).  \n   Then the above inequality gives \\(\\nu_{r}(f(n))\\le 0\\); thus such an \\(r\\) cannot exist.\n\n   Consequently **no prime other than those dividing \\(n\\) can divide \\(f(n)\\)**, and by (1) each prime divisor of \\(n\\) occurs in \\(f(n)\\) with exactly the same exponent as in \\(n\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   This completes the induction.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Every bonza function coincides with the identity function; the constant function \\(f\\equiv 1\\) also satisfies the definition, but it is dominated by the identity.  \n   Therefore for any bonza function  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\le n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N ,\n   \\]\n\n   and the smallest possible constant is \\(c=1\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be a bonza function, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N\\qquad \nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)} .\\tag{*}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value at \\(1\\)\n\nPut \\(a=1\\) in \\((*)\\):\n\n\\[\nf(1)\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\qquad\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nTaking \\(b=1\\) gives \\(f(1)\\mid 1-f(1)^{\\,f(1)}\\).  \nSince \\(f(1)^{\\,f(1)}\\equiv 0\\pmod{f(1)}\\), the right–hand side is congruent to \\(1\\pmod{f(1)}\\); thus \\(f(1)\\mid1\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(1)=1}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Prime arguments\n\nLet \\(p\\) be a prime.  \nFrom \\((*)\\) with \\(a=p,\\;b=p\\) we obtain \\(f(p)\\mid p^{p}\\); hence\n\n\\[\nf(p)=p^{k}\\qquad\\text{for some integer }k\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAssume \\(k\\ge 2\\) and derive a contradiction.  \nChoose the integer \\(b=p+2\\); note that \\(\\gcd(p,p+2)=1\\).  \nSince \\(p\\nmid p+2\\), the prime \\(p\\) does **not** divide \\(f(p+2)\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nf(p+2)^{\\,p^{k}}\\equiv 1\\pmod{p^{k}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApplying \\((*)\\) with \\((a,b)=(p,p+2)\\) and using (3) we get\n\n\\[\np^{k}\\mid (p+2)^{p}-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBy the Lifting‑the‑Exponent Lemma (LTE), for an odd prime \\(p\\)\n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl((p+2)^{p}-1\\bigr)=v_{p}(p+2-1)+v_{p}(p)=0+1=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the right–hand side of (4) is divisible by \\(p\\) but **not** by \\(p^{2}\\); (4) forces \\(k=1\\).  \n(The case \\(p=2\\) is even simpler: \\((2+2)^{2}-1=15\\) is not divisible by \\(4\\).)\n\nHence for every prime \\(p\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(p)=p}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Induction on the argument\n\nWe prove that \\(f(n)=n\\) for all \\(n\\ge 1\\) by strong induction.\n\n*Induction hypothesis.*  \nAssume that for every integer \\(m<n\\) we already have \\(f(m)=m\\).\n\n*Step.*  Let \\(n>1\\) and write \\(n=p^{\\alpha}q\\) where \\(p\\) is a prime divisor of \\(n\\) and \\((p,q)=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The prime divisor \\(p\\) occurs in \\(f(n)\\) with the same exponent\n\nTake \\((a,b)=(p,n)\\) in \\((*)\\).  \nUsing (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\np\\mid n^{p}-f(n)^{p}.\n\\]\n\nReducing modulo \\(p\\) and applying Fermat’s theorem (\\(n^{p}\\equiv n\\pmod p\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\nn\\equiv f(n)\\pmod p .\n\\]\n\nBoth numbers are multiples of \\(p\\); writing \\(n=p^{\\alpha}u,\\;f(n)=p^{\\beta}v\\) with \\((p,u)=(p,v)=1\\) we get \\(p^{\\alpha}u\\equiv p^{\\beta}v\\pmod p\\), whence \\(\\beta\\ge\\alpha\\).  \nSince \\(f(n)\\mid n^{n}\\) (see below) the opposite inequality \\(\\beta\\le\\alpha\\) also holds, so\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p^{\\alpha}\\mid f(n)} .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  No new prime divisor can appear in \\(f(n)\\)\n\nLet \\(r\\) be a prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) with \\(r\\neq p\\).  \nApply \\((*)\\) with \\((a,b)=(n,r)\\).  \nBecause of (5), \\(f(r)=r\\); the divisibility becomes\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid r^{\\,n}-r^{\\,f(n)}=r^{\\,\\min (n,f(n))}\\bigl(r^{|n-f(n)|}-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(r\\nmid r^{|n-f(n)|}-1\\), the whole power of \\(r\\) occurring in \\(f(n)\\) must be contained in the factor \\(r^{\\min (n,f(n))}\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\nu_{r}(f(n))\\le\\min (n,f(n))\\).\n\nIf \\(r\\mid n\\) then, by the induction hypothesis, \\(f(r)=r\\) and \\(r\\) already divides \\(n\\) with the same exponent; (6) shows that the exponent of \\(r\\) in \\(f(n)\\) cannot exceed that in \\(n\\).  \nThus \\(\\nu_{r}(f(n))\\le\\nu_{r}(n)\\).\n\nIf \\(r\\nmid n\\) then \\(\\nu_{r}(n)=0\\) and the inequality forces \\(\\nu_{r}(f(n))=0\\); i.e. such an \\(r\\) cannot exist.\n\nHence **every prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) already divides \\(n\\)** and appears with exactly the same exponent as in \\(n\\). Combining with (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=n}.\n\\]\n\nThis completes the induction step, and therefore\n\n\\[\nf(n)=n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The constant \\(c\\)\n\nThe identity function \\(f(n)=n\\) satisfies the bonza condition, and (8) shows that **any** bonza function coincides with it (the constant function \\(f\\equiv1\\) is also bonza but is dominated by the identity).  \nThus for every bonza function and every \\(n\\)