\\pmod{p^{\\alpha+1}}\\); expanding\n\\((p^{\\alpha}m)^{p}\\) and applying LTE gives  \n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(n^{p}-p^{\\alpha p}\\bigr)=\\alpha+1 .\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we deduce  \n\n\\[\np^{\\beta}\\mid p^{\\alpha+1}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\\beta\\le\\alpha+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow apply \\((a,b)=(p,n/p)\\); note that \\(n/p<p^{\\alpha}m\\) and therefore,\nby minimality of \\(n\\), \\(f(n/p)=n/p\\).  The same reasoning yields  \n\n\\[\np^{\\beta-1}\\mid p^{\\alpha}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\\beta\\ge\\alpha .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) forces \\(\\beta=\\alpha\\).  Hence the exponent of the\nsmallest prime divisor of \\(n\\) in \\(f(n)\\) equals its exponent in \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  No extra prime factors\n\nAssume a prime \\(q\\neq p\\) divides \\(f(n)\\); write \\(f(n)=q^{\\gamma}u\\) with\n\\(q\\nmid u\\).  Using \\((a,b)=(n,q)\\) and (5) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid q^{\\,n}-q^{\\,f(n)}=q^{\\,f(n)}\\bigl(q^{\\,n-f(n)}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(q\\nmid q^{\\,n-f(n)}-1\\), the whole power \\(q^{\\gamma}\\) must be\ncontained in the factor \\(q^{\\,f(n)}\\); this forces \\(\\gamma\\le f(n)\\) but,\nmore importantly, the factor \\(q^{\\,n-f(n)}-1\\) contributes no additional\n\\(q\\).  Consequently \\(\\gamma=0\\); i.e. such a prime \\(q\\) cannot divide\n\\(f(n)\\).\n\nThus **no prime other than those dividing \\(n\\) appears in \\(f(n)\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Determination of \\(f\\)\n\nFrom Sections 4 and 5 we conclude that for every prime \\(p\\) dividing \\(n\\)\nthe exponent of \\(p\\) in \\(f(n)\\) equals its exponent in \\(n\\), and no other\nprime occurs.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nThe identity function clearly satisfies \\((*)\\); therefore every bonza\nfunction is bounded by the identity:\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The optimal constant\n\nSince the identity function itself is bonza, any constant \\(c<1\\) would violate\nthe inequality at \\(n=1\\).  Hence the smallest real constant for which  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le cn\\qquad\\text{holds for all bonza functions }f\\text{ and all }n\n\\]\n\nis  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
