iled Solution  \n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be a bonza function, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N\\qquad \nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)} . \\tag{B}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(f(1)=1\\)\n\nPut \\(a=1\\) in (B).  With \\(k:=f(1)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,k}\\qquad\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(b=1\\) gives \\(k\\mid 1-k^{\\,k}\\).  Because \\(k^{\\,k}\\) is a\nmultiple of \\(k\\), the only possibility is \\(k=1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(1)=1}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Every integer divides its image  \n\nLet \\(p\\) be a prime divisor of a positive integer \\(a\\).  \nApply (B) with \\((a,b)=(p,a)\\).  Since \\(p<a\\), by induction on the\nargument that will be given later we already know \\(f(p)=p\\); using (1) as\nthe base of the induction this is legitimate.  We obtain  \n\n\\[\np\\mid a^{p}-f(a)^{p}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\mid a^{p}\\), the above congruence forces \\(p\\mid f(a)\\).\nSince this holds for every prime divisor \\(p\\) of \\(a\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a\\mid f(a)}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently we may write  \n\n\\[\nf(a)=a\\,g(a),\\qquad g(a)\\in\\mathbb N . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimal counter‑example  \n\nAssume that \\(f\\) is **not** the identity.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nn_{0}:=\\min\\{n\\in\\mathbb N\\mid f(n)\\neq n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy minimality and (2),\n\n\\[\nf(m)=m\\quad\\text{for all }m<n_{0},\\qquad \nf(n_{0})=n_{0}\\,g,\\;g\\ge2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A universal exponent  \n\nLet \\(b\\) be any integer with \\(\\gcd(b,f(n_{0}))=1\\).  \nUsing (B) with \\((a,b)=(n_{0},b)\\) and (4) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})\\mid b^{\\,n_{0}}-b^{\\,f(n_{0})}\n          =b^{\\,n_{0}}\\bigl(1-b^{\\,f(n_{0})-