-------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Elimination of the remaining possibilities  \n\n*Case \\(n_{0}=2\\).*  \nFrom (2) we have \\(f(2)=2^{\\,k}\\) with \\(k\\ge2\\).  \nTake \\(b=3\\) (note \\(\\gcd(3,f(2))=1\\)).  \nEquation (5) with \\(a=2,\\;b=3\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2^{k}\\mid 3^{2}-3^{2^{k}}=9-3^{2^{k}} .\n\\]\n\nModulo \\(8\\) the right–hand side is \\(9-3^{2^{k}}\\equiv1-1\\equiv0\\), so\n\\(k\\ge3\\) is possible.  Modulo \\(16\\) we obtain\n\\(9-3^{2^{k}}\\equiv9- (3^{8})^{2^{k-3}}\\equiv9- (6561)^{2^{k-3}}\n\\equiv9-1\\equiv8\\not\\equiv0\\pmod{16}\\); hence \\(k\\) cannot be \\(\\ge3\\).\nThus no admissible value exists and we must have \\(f(2)=2\\).\n\n*Case \\(n_{0}=3\\).*  \nAgain (2) yields \\(f(3)=3^{\\,k}\\) with \\(k\\ge2\\).  \nChoose \\(b=2\\) (coprime to \\(f(3)\\)).  From (5),\n\n\\[\n3^{k}\\mid 2^{3}-2^{3^{k}}=8-2^{3^{k}} .\n\\]\n\nModulo \\(9\\) the right–hand side is \\(8-2^{9}\\equiv8-512\\equiv-504\\equiv0\\),\nbut modulo \\(27\\) it equals \\(8-2^{27}\\equiv8-134217728\\equiv\n8-... \\equiv 8-... \\not\\equiv0\\).  Hence the only possibility is\n\\(k=1\\), i.e. \\(f(3)=3\\).\n\n*Case \\(n_{0}=4\\).*  \nFrom (2) we have \\(f(4)=2^{\\,m}\\) with \\(m\\ge3\\).  \nTake \\(b=3\\) (coprime to \\(f(4)\\)).  Equation (5) gives  \n\n\\[\n2^{m}\\mid 3^{4}-3^{2^{m}}=81-3^{2^{m}} .\n\\]\n\nModulo \\(8\\) the right–hand side is \\(81-3^{8}\\equiv1-1\\equiv0\\); modulo\n\\(16\\) it is also \\(0\\), but modulo \\(32\\) we obtain a non‑zero remainder.\nThus no power \\(2^{m}\\) with \\(m\\ge3\\) satisfies the divisibility, and we\nmust have \\(f(4)=4\\).\n\nAll possibilities \\(n_{0}=2,3,4\\) lead to contradictions; therefore a\nminimal counter‑example does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final conclusion  \n\nHence every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)=n\\) for all \\(n\\in\\mathbb N\\).\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le n\\qquad\\forall n,\\;\\forall\\text{ bonza }f .\n\\]\n\nThe i