tailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf:\\mathbb N\\longrightarrow\\mathbb N ,\\qquad \n\\text{with}\\qquad \nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\quad\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\tag{B}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The value at \\(1\\)\n\nPut \\(a=1\\) in (B).  Denoting \\(k:=f(1)\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,k}\\qquad\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(b=1\\) gives \\(k\\mid 1-k^{\\,k}\\).  Since the right‑hand side is\ncongruent to \\(1\\pmod{k}\\), we obtain \\(k=1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(1)=1}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A first divisibility\n\nTaking \\(b=a\\) in (B) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid a^{a}-f(a)^{\\,f(a)} .\n\\]\n\nBecause the second term on the right is a multiple of \\(f(a)\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a)\\mid a^{a}}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every prime divisor of \\(f(a)\\) also divides \\(a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimal counter‑example\n\nAssume that \\(f\\) is **not** the constant function \\(1\\) and **not** the\nidentity.  Define  \n\n\\[\nn_{0}:=\\min\\{n\\ge 2\\mid f(n)\\neq1\\text{ and }f(n)\\neq n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe set in (3) is non‑empty by the assumption, so \\(n_{0}\\) is well\ndefined.  For every integer \\(m<n_{0}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(m)=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad f(m)=m . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Behaviour on primes\n\nLet \\(p\\) be a prime divisor of \\(n_{0}\\).  Because \\(p<n_{0}\\), (4) gives  \n\n\\[\nf(p)\\in\\{1,p\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Elimination of the two possibilities for a prime divisor  \n\nWe treat the two possibilities in (5) separately.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.**  There exists a prime divisor \\(p\\mid n_{0}\\) with \\(f(p)=1\\).\n\nInsert \\((a,b)=(n_{0},p)\\) into (B).  Since \\(\\gcd(p,f(n_{0}))=p\\) and\n\\(f(p)=1\\),\n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})\\mid p^{\\,n_{0}}-1 . \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2), every prime factor of \\(f(n_{0})\\) divides \\(n_{0}\\); in\nparticular none of them equals \\(p\\).  Hence the right‑hand side of (6.1) is\ncoprime to \\(f(n_{0})\\), which forces \\(f(n_{0})=1\\).  This contradicts the\ndefinition of \\(n_{0}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.**  For **every** prime divisor \\(p\\mid n_{0}\\) we have \\(f(p)=p\\).\n\nFix such a prime \\(p\\) and again use \\((a,b)=(n_{0},p)\\) in (B).  Because\n\\(f(p)=p\\),\n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})\\mid p^{\\,n_{0}}-p^{\\,f(n_{0})}\n          =p^{\\,f(n_{0})}\\bigl(p^{\\,n_{0}-f(n_{0})}-1\\bigr). \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(f(n_{0})\\neq n_{0}\\) then the exponent \\(n_{0}-f(n_{0})\\) is a non‑zero\ninteger, and the factor \\(p^{\\,n_{0}-f(n_{0})}-1\\) is **not** divisible by\n\\(p\\).  Therefore the exact power of \\(p\\) dividing the right‑hand side of\n(6.2) is \\(p^{\\,f(n_{0})}\\); consequently **all** prime divisors of\n\\(f(n_{0})\\) are equal to \\(p\\).  Since the same argument works for every\nprime divisor of \\(n_{0}\\), the only way this can happen is that \\(n_{0}\\) is\na power of a single prime, say  \n\n\\[\nn_{0}=p^{\\,r}\\qquad(r\\ge1),\\qquad \nf(n_{0})=p^{\\,s}\\qquad(s\\ge1). \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nNow apply (B) with \\((a,b)=(p,p^{\\,r})\\).  Because \\(f(p)=p\\),\n\n\\[\np\\mid (p^{\\,r})^{p}-p^{\\,f(p^{\\,r})}\n   =p^{\\,pr}-p^{\\,p^{\\,s}} .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(p\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np^{\\,pr-1}\\equiv p^{\\,p^{\\,s}-1}\\pmod{p}.\n\\]\n\nBoth sides are congruent to \\(0\\) modulo \\(p\\) unless the exponents are\n\\(1\\); the only way the congruence can hold is when the two exponents are\nequal, i.e. \\(pr=p^{\\,s}\\).  Because \\(p\\) is prime, this forces\n\\(r=s\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})=p^{\\,s}=p^{\\,r}=n_{0},\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the definition of \\(n_{0}\\).\n\n---\n\nBoth cases lead to contradictions; therefore the set defining \\(n_{0}\\) is\nempty.  Hence **no** integer \\(n\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(f(n)\\neq1\\) and\n\\(f(n)\\neq n\\).  We have proved\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N\\;,\\; f(n)=1\\ \\text{or}\\ f(n)=n}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Determination of the optimal constant  \n\nFor the constant function \\(f\\equiv1\\) we have \\(f(n)=1\\le n\\) for every\n\\(n\\).  For the identity function \\(f(n)=n\\) we have equality\n\\(f(n)=n\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{f(n)}{n}\\le 1\\qquad\\text{for every bonza }f\\text{ and every }n,\n\\]\n\nand the value \\(c=1\\) is attained (by the identity function).  No smaller\nreal constant can work, because the identity function would violate the\ninequality.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer:** the smallest real constant satisfying the required\nproperty is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
