hoose an arbitrary odd integer \\(b\\).  Because \\(m\\) is odd, (1) gives \\(f(b)=1\\).  Using the bonza condition with \\((a,b)=(n,b)\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     2^{t}\\mid b^{\\,n}-1\\qquad\\forall\\;b\\text{ odd}. \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n   * For odd \\(b\\) and even exponent \\(n\\), LTE yields  \n\n     \\[\n     v_{2}\\!\\bigl(b^{\\,n}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+v_{2}(n)-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Since one of \\(b-1,\\;b+1\\) is divisible by \\(4\\) and the other by \\(2\\), we always have  \n\n     \\[\n     v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)\\ge 3 .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence for every odd \\(b\\)\n\n     \\[\n     v_{2}\\!\\bigl(b^{\\,n}-1\\bigr)\\ge v_{2}(n)+2 . \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n   * From (2) and (3) we deduce  \n\n     \\[\n     t\\le v_{2}(n)+2 . \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n   * Therefore for any even \\(n\\)\n\n     \\[\n     f(n)=2^{t}\\le 2^{\\,v_{2}(n)+2}=4\\cdot2^{\\,v_{2}(n)}\\le 4n . \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f1 (odd primes never appear).**  \n\n   *Suppose a prime \\(p\\) satisfies \\(f(p)=1\\). Then \\(p\\) does not divide \\(f(m)\\) for any \\(m\\in\\mathbb N\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  Assume the contrary: \\(p\\mid f(m)\\) for some \\(m\\).  Apply the bonza condition with \\((a,b)=(m,p)\\).  Because \\(f(p)=1\\),\n\n     \\[\n     f(m)\\mid p^{\\,m}-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     The left\u2011hand side is a multiple of \\(p\\), while the right\u2011hand side is congruent to \\(-1\\pmod p\\); impossible.  \u220e\n\n   *In particular, for every odd prime \\(p\\) we have \\(f(p)\\neq1\\) only when \\(p\\mid f(p)\\); but then Lemma\u202f1 applied to the prime \\(q\\) dividing \\(p-1\\) (which is odd) forces \\(q\\nmid f(p)\\).  Consequently **no odd prime ever divides any value of a bonza function**; all values are powers of \\(2\\) except possibly at odd arguments, where (1) shows the whole value divides the argument itself.\n\n5. **Putting the two parts together.**  \n\n   * For odd \\(n\\) we have \\(f(n)\\le n\\) by (1).  \n   * For even \\(n\\) we have \\(f(n)\\le 4n\\) by (5).  \n\n   Hence every bonza function satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\le 4n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Sharpness of the constant.**  \n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   f(1)=1,\\qquad \n   f(n)=\\begin{cases}\n        1, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n        2^{\\,v_{2}(n)+2}, & n\\text{ even}.\n        \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   \u2013 For odd \\(b\\) we have \\(f(b)=1\\); for even \\(a\\) the LTE computation in step\u202f3 shows that \\(2^{\\,v_{2}(a)+2}=f(a)\\) divides \\(b^{a}-1\\), i.e. the bonza condition holds.  \n   \u2013 For even \\(b\\) write \\(b=2^{\\ell}u\\) with \\(u\\) odd.  Then \\(f(b)=2^{\\ell+2}\\).  For any \\(a\\) the difference  \n\n     \\[\n     b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}=2^{\\ell a}u^{a}-2^{(\\ell+2)f(a)}\n     \\]\n\n     has 2\u2011adic valuation at least \\(\\min(\\ell a,(\\ell+2)f(a))\\ge f(a)\\); the odd part is a multiple of \\(u^{a}\\) and therefore of \\(a^{a}\\), which is divisible by \\(f(a)\\) by (2).  Hence the whole expression is a multiple of \\(f(a)\\).  \n\n   Thus the function is bonza.  For any power of two \\(n=2^{k}\\;(k\\ge2)\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=2^{k+2}=4n ,\n   \\]\n\n   so the ratio \\(f(n)/n\\) attains the value \\(4\\).  Consequently no constant smaller than \\(4\\) can satisfy the required inequality for **all** bonza functions.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The smallest real constant \\(c\\) such that every bonza function fulfills \\(f(n)\\le cn\\) for all positive integers \\(n\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4}.\n   \\]"
