ined f(n) = 2^{v_2(n)+2} for all even n, which gave f(2)=8. That fails. But we could modify to set f(2)=4. Let's test if this modified function satisfies condition for all a,b. We need to verify for all a,b. Let's attempt to see if any other conflict arises.\n\nDefine f(n) = 1 if n odd; f(2) = 4; for n even with v_2(n) = k ≥ 2, define f(n) = 2^{k+2}. So for n=4 (k=2) => f(4)=2^{4}=16; n=6 (k=1) but we treat k=1? Actually v_2(6)=1, but we have special case for n=2 only; for n=6 (k=1) we would assign f(6)=2^{1+2}=8. So f(6)=8. So for n even with k=1 but n>2, we assign f(n)=8. So the only exception is n=2.\n\nNow we need to verify condition for all a,b. Let's attempt to see if any conflict arises for a=2 (f(a)=4) and b even with f(b)=8 (like b=6). We already tested a=2,b=6: 4 | 6^2 - 8^4 = 36 - 4096 = -4060, divisible by 4. Good.\n\nNow a=2,b=10 (b=10, v_2=1 => f(10)=8). 4 | 10^2 - 8^4 = 100 - 4096 = -3996. -3996/4 = -999, integer. Good.\n\nNow a=2,b=14: 4 | 14^2 - 8^4 = 196 - 4096 = -3900, divisible by 4? -3900/4 = -975, integer. Yes.\n\nThus a=2 seems fine.\n\nNow a=4 (f(4)=16). Need to test b=2 (f(2)=4). Condition: 16 | 2^4 - 4^{16} = 16 - 4^{16}. 4^{16} = (2^2)^{16} = 2^{32}. So 16 - 2^{32} = - (2^{32} - 16). Since 2^{32} is divisible by 16, difference is divisible by 16. Good.\n\nNow a=4,b=6 (f(6)=8). Need 16 | 6^4 - 8^{16}. 6^4 = 1296. 8^{16} = 2^{48}. 2^{48} is divisible by 16. So 1296 - 2^{48} ≡ 1296 (mod 16). 1296 mod 16 = 1296 - 16*81 = 1296 - 1296 = 0. So divisible. Good.\n\nNow a=6 (f(6)=8). Need to test b=4 (f(4)=16). Condition: 8 | 4^6 - 16^8. 4^6 = 4096 = 2^{12}. 16^8 = (2^4)^8 = 2^{32}. So 2^{12} - 2^{32} = -2^{12}(2^{20} - 1). This is divisible by 2^{12}, which is divisible by 8. So condition holds.\n\nNow a=6,b=6: 8 | 6^6 - 8^8. 6^6 = (2*3)^6 = 2^6 * 3^6 = 64 * 729 = 46656. 8^8 = 2^{24} = 16,777,216. Difference = -16,730,560. Divisible by 8? Yes.\n\nThus seems plausible that the modified function works.\n\nBut we need to verify for all a,b. Let's atte