 on even numbers.**  \n   For even \\(a\\) write \\(a=2^{k}m\\) (\\(k\\ge1\\)).  \n   Since every odd \\(b\\) satisfies \\(f(b)=1\\), the bonza condition yields  \n   \\[\n   f(a)\\mid b^{\\,a}-1\\qquad\\forall\\;b\\text{ odd}.\n   \\]  \n   By Lemma\u202f(1) the right\u2011hand side is always a multiple of \\(2^{k+2}\\) and has no odd common divisor. Consequently  \n   \\[\n   f(a)\\mid 2^{\\,k+2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]  \n   Because \\(a=2^{k}m\\ge2^{k}\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   2^{\\,k+2}=4\\cdot2^{\\,k}\\le4a .\n   \\]  \n   Together with (2) this gives the universal bound  \n   \\[\n   f(n)\\le4n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sharpness of the constant.**  \n   Define a function \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) by  \n   \\[\n   f(n)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   1, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   4, & n=2,\\\\[2mm]\n   2^{\\,v_{2}(n)+2}, & n\\ge4\\text{ even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   \u2013 If \\(a\\) is odd, then \\(f(a)=1\\) and the condition holds trivially.  \n\n   \u2013 Let \\(a\\) be even, \\(a=2^{k}m\\) with \\(k\\ge1\\).  \n\n     \u2022 For odd \\(b\\) we have \\(f(b)=1\\); by LTE,\n       \\[\n       v_{2}(b^{\\,a}-1)=v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+k-1\\ge k+2,\n       \\]\n       so \\(2^{k+2}=f(a)\\mid b^{\\,a}-1\\).  \n\n     \u2022 For even \\(b=2^{\\ell}n\\) (\\(\\ell\\ge1,\\ n\\) odd) we have \\(f(b)=2^{\\ell+2}\\) (or \\(f(2)=4\\) when \\(\\ell=1\\)).  \n       The required divisibility is\n       \\[\n       2^{k+2}\\mid (2^{\\ell}n)^{\\,a}-\\bigl(2^{\\ell+2}\\bigr)^{\\,2^{k+2}} .\n       \\]\n       Both terms are multiples of \\(2^{k+2}\\) because \\(\\ell a\\ge2^{k+1}\\ge k+2\\) for \\(k\\ge2\\); the case \\(k=1\\) (i.e. \\(a=2\\)) is checked directly:\n       \\[\n       4\\mid b^{2}-f(b)^{4}\\quad\\text{for every even }b,\n       \\]\n       which holds because each side is congruent to \\(0\\pmod4\\).  \n\n   Hence the function (4) satisfies the bonza condition for all \\(a,b\\).\n\n   For any power of two \\(n=2^{k}\\) with \\(k\\ge2\\) we have \\(f(n)=2^{k+2}=4n\\).  Therefore the inequality \\(f(n)\\le cn\\) cannot hold with any constant \\(c<4\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le4n\\), and the constant \\(4\\) cannot be improved.  Hence the smallest admissible constant is \\(\\boxed{4}\\)."
