's try to prove it ourselves: Suppose there exists an odd b0 such that f(b0) > 1. Then f(b0) is odd >1. Let p be an odd prime dividing f(b0). Since f(b0) | b0^{b0}, p divides b0. So p divides b0. So b0 is divisible by p. So b0 = p * t, where t is integer. Since b0 is odd, p is odd.\n\nNow consider a = p. Then f(p) divides b^p - f(b)^{f(p)} for all b. In particular, consider b = b0. Then f(p) divides b0^p - f(b0)^{f(p)}. Since p divides b0, b0^p is divisible by p^p, but we need to see divisibility by p.\n\nBut maybe we can derive a contradiction. Let's try to see if we can find a bonza function where f(odd) > 1. Maybe the only bonza functions are those where f(odd)=1. The constructed function uses that. Perhaps the condition forces f(odd)=1. Let's attempt to prove.\n\nTake any odd a. Consider b = a+2 (also odd). Then f(a) divides (a+2)^a - f(a+2)^{f(a)}. Since a+2 is odd, f(a+2) is odd (if it's >1). Hard to deduce.\n\nAlternatively, consider b = a^k for some k. Since f(b) divides b^b, f(b) divides (a^k)^{a^k} = a^{k a^k}. So f(b) is a divisor of a^{something}, thus its prime divisors are among primes dividing a. So f(b) is a power of primes dividing a. Since a is odd, f(b) is odd.\n\nThus all f values are odd for odd arguments.\n\nNow consider a odd, b even. Then b^a is even, f(b) is maybe even (if b even). Then f(b)^{f(a)} is even^{odd} = even. So RHS is even - even = even. So f(a) divides an even number. Since f(a) is odd, it must divide the odd part of that even number. So not much restriction.\n\nThus the condition does not seem to force f(odd) = 1 directly.\n\nBut maybe we can combine multiple constraints to force f(odd) = 1. Let's attempt to find a bonza function where f(odd) = 3 for some odd number. Let's try to construct a candidate.\n\nGoal: define f(odd) = some odd divisor of odd^odd, maybe the odd itself. For simplicity, try f(n) = n for all n. Does that satisfy the condition? Let's test: f(a) = a. Then condition: a divides b^a - f(b)^{a} = b^a - b^{a} = 0